48@P8: among the best in keeping memory usage low.\nL749@P8: Using a strategy similar to the one used in this section,\nL750@P8: memory allocation and release patterns causing worst case\nL751@P8: fragmentation and memory usage can be found for other\nL752@P8: types of allocators. For example, the worst case memory\nL753@P8: usage for the binary-buddy system would be similar to the\nL754@P8: one presented in this section for the case where the size\nL755@P8: increment in each cycles is twice the previous size. Thus, if\nL756@P8: cycle i uses a block size of x, then cycle i þ 1 uses a block\nL757@P8: size of 2x and the following cycle a block size of 4x, etc.\nL758@P8: This is so because the binary-buddy allocator always\nL759@P8: allocates blocks in sizes that are powers of 2; moreover,\nL760@P8: coalesced and split blocks are also always powers of 2 in\nL761@P8: size. Thus, there are only log2ðM=nÞ block sizes possible if\nL762@P8: minimum block size is n, and consequently, there are only\nL763@P8: log2ðM=nÞ cycles possible. In each cycle, we can have a\nL764@P8: maximum of M=2 bytes of memory available for allocation\nL765@P8: including memory freed from all previous cycles. This can\nL766@P8: be seen by following a line of reasoning similar to the one\nL767@P8: presented for the first-fit allocator earlier. Thus, memory\nL768@P8: usage can be as high as M þ 0:5M log2ðM=nÞ. However,\nL769@P8: there is also a very large internal fragmentation in the\nL770@P8: binary-buddy system. A request for a block of size p þ 1,\nL771@P8: where p is power of 2 will be rounded up to 2p, the next\nL772@P8: power of 2. The internal fragmentation in the worst case can\nL773@P8: be as high as 50 percent, so an actual program need for\nL774@P8: M=2 bytes can result in a program requirement for M bytes.\nL775@P8: This can double the actual upper bound of memory usage\nL776@P8: of binary-buddy allocators compared to first-fit allocators.\nL777@P8: The worst case fragmentation pattern u